d combine the like terms, simplifying the coefficients as much as possible.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{3}k+1+\\frac{4}{3}k-3 & = \\\\ (\\frac{1}{3}k+\\frac{4}{3}k)+(1-3) & = \\\\ (\\frac{1+4}{3})k+(-2) & = \\\\ \\frac{5}{3}k-2 & \\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186138070 #edb/ref 1069 "e-12836" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186138070 #edb/ref 1071 "Simplify $\\dfrac{1}{2}(p-4q).$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186138070 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "30cf0891-33d3-566c-8286-4c161f84c1dd" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186138070 #edb/ref 1074 "Applying the distributive law and simplifying, we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}(p-4q) & = \\\\ (\\frac{1}{2})\\cdot p-(\\frac{1}{2})\\cdot 4q & = \\\\ \\frac{1}{2}p-\\frac{4q}{2} & = \\\\ \\frac{1}{2}p-2q & \\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186138071 #edb/ref 1069 "e-12837" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186138071 #edb/ref 1071 "Simplify the expression $-\\dfrac{1}{2}u  + \\dfrac{7}{8}u.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186138071 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "53c1f7ca-bcaa-59e4-9929-0695b67621cb" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186138071 #edb/ref 1074 "We combine the like terms by adding the coefficients.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{2}u+\\frac{7}{8}u & = \\\\ (-\\frac{1}{2}+\\frac{7}{8})u & \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn order to add the coefficients, we have to convert them to have the same denominator.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(-\\frac{1}{2}+\\frac{7}{8})u & = \\\\ (-\\frac{4}{8}+\\frac{7}{8})u & = \\\\ (\\frac{-4+7}{8})u & = \\\\ \\frac{3}{8}u & \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, the expression simplifies to $\\dfrac{3}{8}u.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186138072 #edb/ref 1069 "e-12892" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186138072 #edb/ref 1071 "Simplify $\\dfrac{1}{3}(3a+6b)+2a.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186138072 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "957b3102-6eb7-52d3-9299-54ed9bd4718e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186138072 #edb/ref 1074 "First, we apply the distributive law.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{3}(3a+6b)+2a & = \\\\ (\\frac{1}{3})\\cdot 3a+(\\frac{1}{3})\\cdot 6b+2a & = \\\\ \\frac{3a}{3}+\\frac{6b}{3}+2a & = \\\\ a+2b+2a & \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we collect and combine the like terms, simplifying the coefficients as much as possible.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a+2b+2a & = \\\\ a+2a+2b & = \\\\ 3a+2b & \\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186138510 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186050311 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186138510 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186050452 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186138510 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186050833 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186138510 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186138716 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186138510 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186138809 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186138510 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186459206 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186138510 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186459226 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186138510 #edb/ref 1039 "Simplifying Fractions with Like Denominators" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186138510 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186138069 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186138510 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "54498b47-9a43-5b4d-8bbf-806af5968829" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186138511 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186050131 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186138511 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186050637 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186138511 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186138816 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186138511 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186138817 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186138511 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186459205 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186138511 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186459293 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186138511 #edb/ref 1039 "Simplifying Fractions with Unlike Denominators" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186138511 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186138071 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186138511 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "75d096f5-9834-5e52-a7eb-9aae72398d19" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186138512 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592